==Production==
===Development===
[[File:National Memorial Day Concert 2017 (34117818524) (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|Executive producer [[Laurence Fishburne]] in 2017|alt=Terrace smiles, holding a golden Peabody Award trophy]]
During discussions with [[Marvel Studios]] regarding a potential collaboration with Cinema Gypsy Productions for a [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] (MCU) project, president [[Louis D'Esposito]] showed actor and comic book fan [[Laurence Fishburne]] the comic book series ''[[Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur]]''.<ref name="TCA2021">{{Cite web |date=August 19, 2021 |title=Disney Increases Diversity in Children's Animation with "The Ghost and Molly McGee," Marvel's "Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur" and "Rise up, Sing Out" |url=https://www.laughingplace.com/w/articles/2021/08/19/disney-increases-diversity-in-childrens-animation-with-the-ghost-and-molly-mcgee-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-and-rise-up-sing-out/ |access-date=August 21, 2021 |archive-date=August 21, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210821040502/https://www.laughingplace.com/w/articles/2021/08/19/disney-increases-diversity-in-childrens-animation-with-the-ghost-and-molly-mcgee-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-and-rise-up-sing-out/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":11" /> Having read the original ''[[Moon-Boy]] and [[Devil Dinosaur]]'' comic book as a child, ''Moon Girl'' piqued Fishburne's interest. He afterwards began reading the comics, which gave him the inspiration to create an animated series adaptation based on the duo.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |last=Darwish |first=Meaghan |title='Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Team on Marvel Animated Series & Villain Songs |url=https://www.tvinsider.com/1069514/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-disney-channel-new-york-comic-con-2022/ |access-date=November 14, 2022 |website=TV Insider |language=en-US |date=November 13, 2022 |archive-date=November 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221114011846/https://www.tvinsider.com/1069514/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-disney-channel-new-york-comic-con-2022/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="TCA2021" /> Fishburne and a production team worked on the series for two years at [[Disney Television Animation]], before hiring [[Steve Loter]] as executive-producer due to his experience on female-centered shows such as Disney's ''[[Kim Possible]]''.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last=White |first=Abbey |date=October 10, 2022 |title='Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Team on New York-Inspired Animation Style, Diversity in Marvel Series |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/marvel-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-renewed-season-2-nycc-1235236417/ |access-date=October 17, 2022 |website=The Hollywood Reporter |archive-date=October 16, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221016181812/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/marvel-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-renewed-season-2-nycc-1235236417/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":11" /> Loter and the team then created a roller-skating sequence played over "[[Sweatpants (Childish Gambino song)|Sweatpants]]" by [[Childish Gambino]] as a proof-of-concept piece and pitched it to both executives at Disney Television Animation and a Marvel Studios team including Brad Winderbaum and president [[Kevin Feige]], who greenlit the series for development.<ref name=":9" /><ref name=":24" />

On February 20, 2018, it was reported that [[Marvel Animation]] and Cinema Gypsy Productions were developing a ''Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur'' series for [[Disney Kids & Family|Disney Channel Worldwide]].<ref name="Announcement">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=February 20, 2018 |title='Marvel's Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur' Animated Series Produced By Laurence Fishburne In Works At Disney Channels |url=https://deadline.com/2018/02/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-based-comic-book-series-tv-disney-channels-worldwide-1202296647/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224121127/https://deadline.com/2018/02/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-based-comic-book-series-tv-disney-channels-worldwide-1202296647/ |archive-date=February 24, 2021 |access-date=December 14, 2019 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> Fishburne (a long-time Marvel alumni; having played [[Silver Surfer]] in ''[[Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer]]'' and [[Bill Foster (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Bill Foster]] in ''[[Ant-Man and the Wasp]]'') and Helen Sugland serve as the series' executive producers.<ref name="Announcement" /> 20 half-hours were ordered for the first season, but it was later cut down to 17.<ref>{{cite web |work=Deadline| url=https://deadline.com/2020/08/laurence-fishburne-signs-caa-1203017971/ |author=Andreeva, Nellie| title=Laurence Fishburne Signs with CAA for Representation | date=August 19, 2020 | access-date=March 4, 2023 | archive-date=March 4, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230304173758/https://deadline.com/2020/08/laurence-fishburne-signs-caa-1203017971/ | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite tweet|number=1646356161011277825|user=steveloter|title=The production number on OMG Part 2 was 120, because we originally had 20 or so episodes planned....|author=Steve Loter|date=April 12, 2023|access-date=April 14, 2023}}</ref>
 
On August 24, 2019, during the [[D23 (Disney)|D23]] Expo, Fishburne revealed that the series would premiere in 2020 on [[Disney Channel]], with Disney Television Animation being set to co-produce the series, marking the first time Disney and Marvel worked together on an animated series.<ref name="DisneyChannel">{{Cite web |last=Dinh |first=Christine |date=August 24, 2019 |title=D23 Expo 2019: Disney Channel Greenlights Original Animated Series 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/d23-disney-channel-greenlights-animated-series-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210210185027/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/d23-disney-channel-greenlights-animated-series-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur |archive-date=February 10, 2021 |access-date=December 14, 2019 |website=[[Marvel.com]]}}</ref> Loter executive produces the series alongside Fishburne and Sugland;<ref name="DisneyChannel" /> Loter also serves as the series' [[showrunner]].<ref name=":23" /> Jeff Howard and Kate Kondell work on the series as co-producers and story editors.<ref name="DisneyChannel" /> Howard and Kondell were the first two writers hired for the series. The two helped the production team define the characters, with an hour being dedicated to a specific character. By the time writing for the episodes started, the characters were defined enough that not many ideas were revised.<ref name=":15" /> Kondell also serves as head writer for the series.<ref>{{Cite tweet|number=1633195816973369344|user=DisneyTVA|title=To celebrate #WomensHistoryMonth, #MoonGirlAndDevilDinosaur Head Writer Kate Kondell will be answering your questions, leave them in the replies for her to answer!...|author=Disney TVA|date=March 7, 2023|access-date=March 8, 2023}}</ref>
 
Fishburne said that "Disney Channel is the perfect platform to explore this pint-sized female African American superhero and [he] can't wait for their audience to enjoy the lighthearted adventures of Lunella and Devil Dinosaur",<ref name="DisneyChannel" /> while [[Cort Alan Lane|Cort Lane]], Marvel Family and Entertainment's then-senior vice president, said that "[Moon Girl's] adventures with giant buddy, Devil Dinosaur, are filled with so much wonder and joy, and this historic partnership with Disney Television Animation and Cinema Gypsy Productions proved the right formula to bring them to television".<ref name="DisneyChannel" />

In February 2021, it was reported that Rodney Clouden would serve as supervising producer for the series, while Pilar Flynn would produce.<ref name=cast/> Due to Disney Television Animation's studios having closed in 2020 in response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]],<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Andreeva |first1=Nellie |last2=Patten |first2=Dominic |date=March 24, 2020 |title=Animation Production Is Still Going, Sometimes Slower, Amid Coronavirus Crisis |url=https://deadline.com/2020/03/animation-tv-series-continue-coronavirus-challenges-1202890786/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227083655/https://deadline.com/2020/03/animation-tv-series-continue-coronavirus-challenges-1202890786/ |archive-date=February 27, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood|Deadline]]}}</ref> most of the series was produced remotely.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bricken |first=Rob |date=February 11, 2021 |title=The Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur Cartoon Just Got a Great Voice Cast and a Surprising Marvel Character |url=https://www.gizmodo.com.au/2021/02/the-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-cartoon-just-got-a-great-voice-cast-and-a-surprising-marvel-character/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210211040503/https://www.gizmodo.com.au/2021/02/the-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-cartoon-just-got-a-great-voice-cast-and-a-surprising-marvel-character/ |archive-date=February 11, 2021 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |website=[[Gizmodo]]}}</ref> The producers developed each episode with a feature film-like pipeline, with each script receiving a "color script" as with films, and each episode being treated as a "mini-movie".<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Reif |first=Alex |date=October 23, 2022 |title=LightBox Expo Recap: "Introducing Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur" |url=https://www.laughingplace.com/w/articles/2022/10/23/lightbox-expo-recap-introducing-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=October 24, 2022 |website=LaughingPlace.com |language=en-US |archive-date=March 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326032919/https://www.laughingplace.com/w/articles/2022/10/23/lightbox-expo-recap-introducing-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":15" /> The producers wanted the team to be composed of people with different filmmaking experiences.<ref name=":15">{{Cite web |date=February 3, 2023 |title=Rodney Clouden and Steve Loter |url=https://open.spotify.com/episode/7dfVcMxNI4lnNsd29Iu30d |access-date=February 5, 2023 |website=[[Spotify]] |language=en |archive-date=February 5, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230205212822/https://open.spotify.com/episode/7dfVcMxNI4lnNsd29Iu30d |url-status=live }}</ref>

In October 2022, the series was renewed for a second season.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=NYCC 2022: 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Will Be Back with Season 2 |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/nycc-2022-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-season-2 |access-date=October 8, 2022 |website=Marvel Entertainment |language=en |archive-date=June 1, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601123937/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/nycc-2022-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-season-2 |url-status=live }}</ref> The series was renewed after a positive reception from both Disney and Marvel executives.<ref name=":0" /> Rafael Chaidez took over as producer for season two, while Flynn and Clouden serve as co-executive producers alongside Kondell. Kondell also serves as story editor alongside Halima Lucas, the latter who also became a co-producer alongside Ben Juwono, who replaced Clouden as supervising director.<ref name=":2" /> Flynn was also promoted to co-showrunner alongside Loter.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ruby |first=Ayla |date=2023-02-10 |title=Interview with Pilar Flynn from Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur |url=https://thecosmiccircus.com/cosmic-cafe-interview-with-pilar-flynn-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=2024-04-18 |website=The Cosmic Circus |language=en-US |archive-date=March 10, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240310185937/https://thecosmiccircus.com/cosmic-cafe-interview-with-pilar-flynn-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Production for season 2 began by February 2023.<ref name=":15" />

Loter said that the production team envisions the series to span three seasons, believing they could tell a complete story within that timeframe while remaining open to the possibility of creating more seasons.<ref>{{Cite tweet|number=1750030197527830678|user=steveloter|title=We do too. We feel like we could tell a very full story in 3 seasons (like a movie trilogy) but could certainly make more.|author=Steve Loter|date=January 24, 2024|access-date=January 24, 2024}}</ref> He also mentioned that the show's renewal or cancellation depends on the ratings of the season 2 premiere on both Disney Channel and [[Disney+]].<ref>{{Cite tweet|number=1750005375498379658|user=steveloter|title=The reality of the situation is this. We already know what to do for a 3rd season of #moongirlanddevildinosaur and it's really, really great. We NEED more viewers to watch the premiere of MG Season Two on Feb 2nd on Disney Channel and Feb 3rd on Disney Plus to make this happen.|author=Steve Loter|date=January 23, 2024|access-date=January 24, 2024}}</ref>

===Writing===
The series is composed entirely of women of color in its writers' room, as well as on its directing team, as the producers wanted the production team to reflect the diversity within [[New York City]], where the show is set.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":3" /> The writers, alongside other crew members of color, were allowed to provide feedback regarding representation of minorities within the show, with some of said crew members receiving promotions within the crew while working on the series.<ref name=":9" /> The crew also worked alongside Disney production coordinator Allen March to identify moments to add diversity to the show via background or speaking characters, as well as to calculate the representation per episode.<ref name=":3" /> Loter, who lives in New York City, said that it was important for the crew to "make sure they've got New York right", wanting to respect the city's "vibe" and "tone".<ref name=":0" /> He also reused elements of the series' portrayal of NYC from his cancelled ''[[Cars (franchise)|Cars]]'' spin-off film ''Metro'',<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1623200347169918978 |user=steveloter |title=#MoonGirlAndDevilDinosaur started with my unfinished Cars World film, Metro.... |author=Steve Loter |date=February 8, 2023 |access-date=February 8, 2023}}</ref> such as prominently featuring the [[City Hall station (IRT Lexington Avenue Line)|New York City Hall station]], which appears in the series as Lunella's lab.<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1623528728843399169 |user=steveloter |title=#MoonGirlAndDevilDinosaur making of fun fact #2- Lunella's lab is a real place!... |author=Steve Loter |date=February 8, 2023 |access-date=February 8, 2023}}</ref> The series starts off with an episodic format as it explores Lunella becoming Moon Girl, before transitioning into a more serialized story, with an arc unfolding throughout the first season, and continuing on into the second season, being more serialized than the first.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1619095654051225600 |user=steveloter |title=We start a little episodic as Lunella gets to become Moon Girl, but we get serialized early. Thanks! |author=Steve Loter |date=January 27, 2023 |access-date=January 27, 2023}}</ref>

Supervising producer Rodney Clouden said it was important for the production crew to accurately portray a multigenerational African-American family.<ref name=":9">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eQBwnYQlDF0 |title=October 10, 2022 |date=October 10, 2022 |publisher=Alexander Yarde |access-date=December 26, 2022 |via=YouTube |archive-date=December 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221226185320/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eQBwnYQlDF0 |url-status=live }}</ref> They wanted to show both Moon Girl learning from her family and her family learning from her through their multiple perspectives.<ref name=":9" /> Each character was given a different personality and profession, such as Pops being owner of a roller-skate and Andria being a DJ, to further explore diversity and how "everyone is so different [from each other]".<ref name=":10">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xrbvq23R-N8 |title=#nycc2022 "Marvel's Moon Girl & Devil Dinosaur Pilar Flynn (producer) & Gary Anthony Williams (Pops) |date=October 10, 2022 |publisher=Alexander Yarde |access-date=December 26, 2022 |via=YouTube |archive-date=December 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221226192222/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xrbvq23R-N8 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Sasheer Zamata]], who voices Lunella's mother, said that family is a crucial theme in the show, and described the series as "a nice way to see a Black family work together and enjoy each other and spread love in their community".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yates |first=Shaquine |date=July 7, 2022 |title=How 'Marvel's Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur' Aims To Take Representation in Animation To Greater Heights |url=https://shadowandact.com/amp/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-is-all-about-representation |website=Shadow and Act |access-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102000746/https://shadowandact.com/amp/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-is-all-about-representation |url-status=live }}</ref> Producer Pilar Flynn said it was important for the writers to have Moon Girl and Casey have a close and supportive relationship, which is rarely seen among female friendships in animation.<ref name=":10" />

The producers used the original comic as "a springboard" and "a point of reference" for the series, although making several alterations regarding the source material to make the story fit in a television format while still incorporating nods to multiple comic-book storylines.<ref name=":9" /> According to [[Diamond White]], the series features "levels of emotions" that were absent from the original comic.<ref name=":4" /> The series portrays the relationship between the duo as "a partnership" instead of "a pet/owner dynamic", with Devil becoming fiercely loyal to Moon Girl and feeling "at home with her" due to how she treats him.<ref name=":4" /> Another changes were the increased role of Lunella's family and the addition of her friend Casey, which were made to showcase how Lunella's world changes as she grows through the series.<ref name=":9" /> They also felt it was important for Lunella to have a support system, and for her household to represent that of many audiences' households.<ref name=":10" /> The producers changed Lunella's age from 9 to 13-years-old because they wanted social media to play a prominent role in her character, which they felt would not work with her being 9-years-old.<ref name=":21">{{Cite news |last=Jackson |first=Leigh-Ann |date=February 8, 2023 |title=A New Marvel Hero Battles Supervillains and Teen Angst |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/08/arts/television/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur.html |access-date=February 10, 2023 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=February 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230210003406/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/08/arts/television/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The series features several obscure characters from Marvel comics. The characters included were selected due to potential roles they could play in the story, interest from the producers, and/or recommendations from Marvel, such as the [[Beyonder]], who was suggested by Marvel Studios president [[Kevin Feige]] as a foil for Lunella;<ref name=":23">{{Cite web |last=Jackson |first=Destiny |date=February 11, 2023 |title='Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Bosses Discuss Bringing The First Black Female Superhero Animated Series To Life: "It's Black Girl Magic" |url=https://deadline.com/2023/02/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-disney-steve-loter-rodney-clouden-1235256811/ |access-date=February 12, 2023 |website=Deadline |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":0" /> the producers were particularly interested in featuring characters that hadn't been adapted to other media before, such as [[Aftershock (comics)|Aftershock]], as their obscurity allowed the creators to make changes that fitted within the show's narrative,<ref name=":0" /> though more prominent Marvel characters are also set to appear. Fishburne noted, however, that some characters were unable to appear due to "proprietary issues",<ref name=":16">{{Cite web |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Moon Girl & Devil Dinosaur Cast and Crew on Marvel's New Animated Hero |url=https://gizmodo.com/marvel-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-disney-cast-ep-intervie-1850051584 |access-date=February 6, 2023 |website=Gizmodo |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":21" /> such as [[Amadeus Cho]], whom the team wanted to include in season 1 but were forbidden by Marvel; elements of the team's plans for the character were ultimately used for [[Kid Kree]] in season 2.<ref name=":24">{{cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E4DvdFreFt0|title=Marvel's Moon Girl Interview (Co-Producer/Sup. Director Ben Juwono) {{!}} Guardians of the Palaxy Pod|publisher=Guardians of the Palaxy|via=YouTube|date=January 31, 2024|access-date=January 31, 2024|archive-date=February 1, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240201002753/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E4DvdFreFt0|url-status=live}}</ref> Elements from Marvel comics appeared more prominently later in the series, after Lunella and "her world" were properly established.<ref name=":16" /> Some of the villains represent real-life issues within the show, such as Aftershock draining the Lower East Side's energy in the first episode, which serves as "a statement" about [[gentrification]].<ref name=":19" /> The series was also described as "complimentary" to the MCU, with the creators including multiple nods to the franchise through the series.<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1550238281622642688 |user=DisneyMGDD |title=#MoonGirlAndDevilDinosaur is complimentary to the MCU and we may be able to spot some Easter eggs in the series 👀 #SDCC2022 |author=Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur |date=July 21, 2022 |access-date=July 22, 2022}}</ref> Fishburne claimed that the series is not "connected to the MCU officially", while Loter revealed that "a couple of MCU characters" would appear in the series.<ref name=":18">{{Cite web |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Inside Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur show coming to Disney Channel and Disney+ this month |url=https://www.thepopverse.com/marvel-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-interview |access-date=February 6, 2023 |website=Popverse |language=en}}</ref> Additionally, Flynn stated that the producers consider the series a "companion series" to the MCU but not part of the franchise.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ruby |first=Ayla |date=2023-02-10 |title=Interview with Pilar Flynn from Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur |url=https://thecosmiccircus.com/cosmic-cafe-interview-with-pilar-flynn-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=2024-03-10 |website=The Cosmic Circus |language=en-US |archive-date=March 10, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240310185937/https://thecosmiccircus.com/cosmic-cafe-interview-with-pilar-flynn-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Casting===
In February 2021, it was reported that White and Tatasciore would voice the titular duo, with Woodard, Williams, Zamata, and Fowler providing the voices of Lunella/Moon Girl's grandparents and parents, respectively, and Barer as her best friend, Casey.<ref name=cast/> It was also reported that Fishburne would voice [[Beyonder|The Beyonder]] in a recurring capacity.<ref name=cast/> Additional recurring and guest roles were announced at [[San Diego Comic-Con]] in July 2022.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=Wesley Snipes, Alison Brie to Guest on Disney's 'Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur': See First Look (Exclusive)|url=https://www.etonline.com/wesley-snipes-alison-brie-to-guest-on-disneys-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-see-first-look-exclusive|website=[[Entertainment Tonight]]|author=Philiana Ng|date=July 21, 2022|access-date=July 21, 2022|archive-date=July 28, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220728092748/https://www.etonline.com/wesley-snipes-alison-brie-to-guest-on-disneys-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-see-first-look-exclusive|url-status=live}}</ref> White was cast shortly after Loter heard her audition tape; according to Loter, her tape was the only one he heard, as he was impressed with her performance.<ref name=":15" />

Voice recordings for season one were partially done remotely due to the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=October 8, 2022 |title=Watch: Diamond White and Fred Tatasciore lead the Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur panel for Marvel |url=https://www.thepopverse.com/watch-diamond-white-and-fred-tatasciore-lead-the-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-panel-for-marvel |access-date=October 8, 2022 |website=Popverse |language=en |archive-date=October 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221008203850/https://www.thepopverse.com/watch-diamond-white-and-fred-tatasciore-lead-the-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-panel-for-marvel |url-status=dead }}</ref> Williams, whose work on the show was his first role during the pandemic, received help from his wife to prepare to record his lines from their home.<ref name=":0" /> White and Barer ad-libbed several dialogues between Moon Girl and Casey,<ref name=":0" /> with the two recording their lines simultaneously either physically or through Zoom.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EHOG1f516Zg |title=Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur Cast & Creators: Is It In The MCU? MCU Cameos?! AND MORE! |date=February 6, 2023 |publisher=Mama's Geeky |access-date=February 6, 2023 |via=YouTube}}</ref> White also recorded some of her lines with Tatatsciore,<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Evzli37xeDc |title=Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur - Friendship And Community {{!}} FEATURETTE |date=February 7, 2023 |publisher=Disney Television Animation News |access-date=February 8, 2023 |via=YouTube |archive-date=February 22, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230222002229/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Evzli37xeDc |url-status=live }}</ref> as well as with Woodward, Zamata, and Williams.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SHNuRZyj0LI |title=Meet the Family Featurette {{!}} Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur {{!}} @disneychannel |date=February 6, 2023 |publisher=Disney Channel |access-date=February 8, 2023 |via=YouTube |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207212853/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SHNuRZyj0LI |url-status=live }}</ref> While providing the vocal effects for Devil, Tatasciore was given a script with lines for him, which he would later work into animal vocals.<ref name=":4">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O187H790jkc |title=Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur's Diamond White & Fred Tatasciore Talk Representation & More - NYCC |date=October 9, 2022 |publisher=The Koalition |access-date=October 18, 2022 |via=YouTube |archive-date=October 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221018134158/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O187H790jkc |url-status=live }}</ref> The idea of Fishburne voicing the Beyonder was suggested by Loter.<ref name=":17">{{Cite web |last=Zachary |first=Brandon |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Marvel's Moon Girl & Devil Dinosaur: Laurence Fishburne & Steve Loter Discuss the Show's Artistic Influences |url=https://www.cbr.com/moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-laurence-fishburne-steve-loter-interview/ |access-date=February 6, 2023 |website=CBR |language=en |archive-date=February 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206181029/https://www.cbr.com/moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-laurence-fishburne-steve-loter-interview/ |url-status=live }}</ref> For his performance, Fishburne wanted it to be lighter and less recognizable than his previous roles, so he and Loter worked together to develop his voice for the Beyonder.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":17" />

===Animation===
The series is animated by [[Flying Bark Productions]], in [[Sydney]], [[Australia]].<ref name="Announcement" /><ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1379968314550575104 |user=flyingbark |title=We're DELIGHTED to announce that Flying Bark is working alongside DisneyTVAnimation & Marvel Animation on their latest series, Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur!... |author=Flying Bark |date=April 7, 2021 |access-date=October 9, 2022}}</ref> Titmouse worked on early visual development for the series.<ref name="Announcement" /> Flying Bark, whose employees were fans of the source material, was hired due to the studio's work on ''[[Rise of the Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles]]''.<ref name=":5" /> Animation lead Kat Kosmala created a series of rules for the animators, among them being that the character animation must draw the viewers' attention, the action must be "simple and direct", and simplification should be used only for designs.<ref name=":5" /> The visual style is meant to "[lean] heavy into [a] 2D graphic comicbook sensibility".<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1585147834508402696 |user=e1n |title=Since our design leans heavy into the 2D graphic comicbook sensibility, we thought it would be cool to create a sequence that deliberately avoids movement toward or away from camera, that way everything feels like a drawing on paper when you're reading a comicbook page. |author=Ben Juwono |date=October 26, 2022 |access-date=November 14, 2022}}</ref> The animation combines hand-drawn animation with [[Toon Boom Harmony]], with Toon Boom being used for stage and head-rigging, while the bodies of the characters are hand-drawn. The producers originally planned to only use Toon Boom, but ultimately felt hand-drawn animation was also required.<ref name=":15" />

Multiple animation styles were used per episode.<ref name=":5" /> For the New York sequences, the producers drew inspiration from graffiti artists, as well as artists such as [[Andy Warhol]], for the series' art style, to portray New York in a more realistic style than most animated shows, while also portraying the city as its own character.<ref name=":0" /> Loter and Clouden drew inspiration from their childhood for the series' portrayal of NYC.<ref name=":15" /> Graffiti artists were hired to work on the graffiti depicted through the show to show accurate graffiti.<ref name=":0" /> The camera movement was also meant to evoke the feeling of a real-life city recording, with "forced tight shots" and "object obstruction".<ref name=":5" /> The buildings and streets were also drawn to be accurate to their real-life counterparts.<ref name=":14">{{Cite web |last=McLean |first=Tom |date=February 3, 2023 |title=Meet the Heroic Girl Genius and Jurassic Sidekick of 'Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' |url=https://www.animationmagazine.net/2023/02/meet-the-heroic-girl-genius-and-jurassic-sidekick-of-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=February 4, 2023 |website=Animation Magazine |language=en-US |archive-date=February 3, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230203230631/https://www.animationmagazine.net/2023/02/meet-the-heroic-girl-genius-and-jurassic-sidekick-of-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The musical sequences feature a more abstract art style that is more colorful and action-focused.<ref name=":5" /> Flashback sequences feature a simpler graphic style with colors that "quickly enhance emotional beats".<ref name=":5" />

Due to Fishburne's love of comic books,<ref name=":18" /> producers also wanted the animation to "feel like an illustrated moving comic book",<ref name=":0" /> so the artists drew inspiration from comic books from the 1970s, featuring a lack of "perfect shapes" and "edgy line work", while having a hand-drawn feel.<ref name=":5" /> Details such as black spots were also added so they could "[feel] like a very complete piece" even before adding color.<ref name=":14" /> Artists wanted to avoid using digital techniques such as motion blur, wanting instead to rely mainly on ink-and-paint techniques.<ref name=":20">{{Cite web |last=Fleshman |first=Cu |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Q&A: Ben Juwono Brings the Magic to Marvel's 'Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' |url=https://charactermedia.com/qa-ben-juwono-brings-the-magic-to-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=February 7, 2023 |website=Character Media |language=en-US |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207010422/https://charactermedia.com/qa-ben-juwono-brings-the-magic-to-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The animation also drew inspiration from ''[[Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse]]'', with the producers wanting the animation to be as energetic as possible within a budget for a TV series. The animators also sought for the animation to be "complimentary" to ''Into the Spider-Verse'' without recycling its animation style.<ref name=":15" /><ref name=":5" /> [[Marvel Studios Animation]]'s ''[[What If...? (TV series)|What If...?]]'' was also an influence on the series' visual design.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web |title=Double Trouble |url=https://pocketmags.com/it/sfx-magazine/february-2023/articles/1264675/double-trouble |access-date=January 26, 2023 |website=pocketmags.com}}</ref>

Lead character designer Jose Lopez wanted the characters to have multiple shapes that were "fun to look at", such as Moon Girl's silhouette changing when she dons her superhero outfit.<ref name=":5" /> He also wanted Devil Dinosaur to look like "a mean dinosaur" while also being "believable as a lovable pet".<ref name=":5" /> He also drew inspiration from comic strip artist [[Bill Watterson]], of whom Lopez is a fan of.<ref name=":5" /> [[Marc Hempel]] and Devil's co-creator [[Jack Kirby]] were also inspirations for the art style of the show, with the artists incorporating the [[Kirby Krackle]] into the series.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Interview: 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Cast and Creatives on Family, Lunella, and Bringing a Fresh Story to the Screen |url=https://www.nerdsandbeyond.com/2023/02/06/interview-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-cast-and-creatives-on-family-lunella-and-bringing-a-fresh-story-to-the-screen/ |access-date=February 6, 2023 |website=Nerds and Beyond |language=en |archive-date=February 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206170006/https://www.nerdsandbeyond.com/2023/02/06/interview-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-cast-and-creatives-on-family-lunella-and-bringing-a-fresh-story-to-the-screen/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The Colorful and Loving World of MOON GIRL AND DEVIL DINOSAUR |url=https://nerdist.com/article/moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-tv-series-influences-themes-of-family-friendship-interview-laurence-fishburne/ |access-date=February 6, 2023 |website=Nerdist |date=February 6, 2023 |language=en-US |archive-date=February 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206185819/https://nerdist.com/article/moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-tv-series-influences-themes-of-family-friendship-interview-laurence-fishburne/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The artists also incorporated elements to visually represent the characters' feelings, such as manga-style "flairs",<ref name=":20" /> as well as emojis that were "simple and clear and direct".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Silverio |first=Ben F. |date=February 6, 2023 |title=Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur Producers Discuss The Feel Of Marvel Animation's New Show [Exclusive Interview] |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/1095933/moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-producers-discuss-the-feel-of-marvel-animations-new-show-exclusive-interview/ |access-date=February 7, 2023 |website=/Film |language=en-US |archive-date=February 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206181959/https://www.slashfilm.com/1095933/moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-producers-discuss-the-feel-of-marvel-animations-new-show-exclusive-interview/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Music===
In July 2022, it was announced that [[Raphael Saadiq]] would serve as the executive music producer for the show.<ref name=":6">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ChqOj8zG5g |title=Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur {{!}} Raphael Saadiq Music Featurette |date=July 1, 2022 |publisher=Marvel Entertainment |access-date=July 1, 2022 |via=YouTube}}</ref> He also provided both the score and songs for the show.<ref name=":0" /> Loter, a fan of Saadiq, approached him to work on the project during an autograph signing.<ref name=":6" /> Each episode features a different song by Saadiq, depicted in-universe as part of a mix-tape Moon Girl listens to; the songs were written before animation work began so the animators could synchronize the songs to the sequence.<ref name=":5" /> Saadiq included different musical genres in the soundtrack to reflect the diversity within New York City.<ref name=":6" /> Producer Pilar Flynn described his score as "sophisticated" and "unlike anything [she has] seen in animation before", saying it "elevated the entire show to another level".<ref name=":10" /> According to Flynn, the crew generally pitched ideas for the score to Saadiq, only for him to discard them in favor of something different, which the producers received positively.<ref name=":10" /> The first episode features a song titled "Where You Come From", which Loter described as "a love letter to New York".<ref name=":0" /> Saadiq also wrote songs for the series' villains.<ref name=":7" />

The show's theme song, "Moon Girl Magic", was performed written by Saadiq alongside Halima Lucas and [[Taura Stinson]], and performed by White.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |title=Opening Theme Song from 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Released {{!}} Film Music Reporter |url=http://filmmusicreporter.com/2022/11/14/opening-theme-song-from-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-released/ |access-date=November 14, 2022 |language=en-US |archive-date=November 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221114100254/https://filmmusicreporter.com/2022/11/14/opening-theme-song-from-marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-released/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":6" /> The song was written by Saadiq with "a similar energy" to "[[Juice (Lizzo song)|Juice]]" by [[Lizzo]], and received an "instant green-light" by the executives, which is rare for most theme songs for a television series.<ref name=":5" /> Saadiq said he wanted the song to be both "uplifting" and "happy", while White was excited to "bring Lunella's voice to the show in a singing way".<ref name=":6" /> The title sequence was storyboarded by supervising director Ben Juwono, who drew inspiration from the music video for [[Cyndi Lauper]]'s "[[Girls Just Want to Have Fun]]" and storyboarded it to play to "Juice", as the theme song had not been written by that point.<ref name=":5" /> The song was released as a single on November 14, 2022.<ref name=":8" />

A soundtrack featuring selected songs from the first season was released on February 10, 2023.<ref name=":8" />

===Censorship===
An episode from Season 2, entitled "[[The Gatekeeper (Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur)|The Gatekeeper]]", was shelved from release; however, the full episode leaked on November 15, 2024. The episode revolved around the openly-[[transgender]] character Brooklyn.<ref name="McWhertor" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pulliam-Moore |first=Charles |date=2024-11-15 |title=Disney has reportedly pulled a Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur episode for telling a trans-inclusive story |url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/11/15/24297438/moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-the-gatekeeper |access-date=2024-11-15 |website=[[The Verge]] |language=en}}</ref> James Whitbrook of ''[[io9]]'', after reviewing the leaked footage, noted that "the episode is clearly not subtle" with its focus on Brooklyn and "the explicit prejudice she faces as a trans teen who wants to [[Transgender people in sports|participate in school sports]]".<ref name="io9 Whitbrook">{{Cite web |last=Whitbrook |first=James |date=2024-11-15 |title=Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur Episode Allegedly Scrubbed Over Trans Storyline |url=https://gizmodo.com/moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-season-2-censorship-trans-episode-disney-2000525143 |access-date=2024-11-15 |website=[[Gizmodo]] |publisher=[[io9]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Artists who worked on series alleged that the episode was pulled as a result of the political climate surrounding [[Donald Trump]] winning the [[2024 United States presidential election]] and the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] winning both chambers of [[United States Congress|Congress]].<ref name="McWhertor">{{cite news |last1=McWhertor |first1=Michael |title=Disney reportedly pulls Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur episode over trans athlete story |url=https://www.polygon.com/news/479614/disney-reportedly-pulls-marvels-moon-girl-and-dinosaur-episode-over-trans-athlete-story |access-date=November 15, 2024 |work=Polygon |date=November 15, 2024}}</ref><ref name="io9 Whitbrook" /><ref name="Collider Mullinax"/> Michael McWhertor of ''[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]'' reported that a Disney source stated the episode was "held" instead of banned, with that determination occurring "more than a year ago and was not based the result of recent U.S. elections".<ref name="McWhertor" /> Whitbrook reported that a Disney source claimed the intent of the hold was "to ensure material doesn't potentially push ahead discussions around social issues before families can have them themselves", however, Whitbrook also commented that "it would not be the first time Disney has capitulated to right-wing systems of power by censoring its own material".<ref name="io9 Whitbrook" /> Despite being unreleased, the episode won a [[Velma Awards|Velma Award]] from the non-profit LGBT rights organization [[Rainbow Project|The Rainbow Project]] the following month.<ref>{{Cite web |last=White |first=Abbey |date=2024-12-10 |title='Heartstopper,' 'Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur,' Among Inaugural Winners of The Velma Awards (Exclusive) |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/heartstopper-primos-monster-high-velma-awards-winners-1236080133/ |access-date=2024-12-11 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |language=}}</ref>

==Release==
''Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur'' premiered on [[Disney Channel]] on February 10, 2023.<ref name="PremiereDate">{{Cite web |last1=Hipes |first1=Patrick |last2=Pederson |first2=Erik |date=September 10, 2022 |title=D23 Expo 2022: All The Movie & TV News We Learned From Marvel, Lucasfilm, Pixar, Disney+ And More |url=https://deadline.com/2022/09/d23-expo-2022-recap-what-we-learned-1235114097/ |access-date=September 11, 2022 |website=Deadline Hollywood |archive-date=November 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221120095720/https://deadline.com/2022/09/d23-expo-2022-recap-what-we-learned-1235114097/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The series was originally scheduled to premiere in 2020,<ref name="DisneyChannel" /> before being delayed to 2022,<ref name="cast" /> and later to its eventual premiere date.<ref name=":1" /> The first six episodes of the series were added to Disney+ on February 15, 2023, resulting in episodes 3 to 6 being released prior to their television debuts.<ref name="Disney Plus release" />

===Marketing===
A teaser trailer showing clips from the opening was released by executive-producer Laurence Fishburne on December 15, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |title='Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Releases First Teaser Trailer |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-teaser-trailer |access-date=November 23, 2022 |website=Marvel Entertainment |language=en |archive-date=November 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221123005859/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-teaser-trailer |url-status=live }}</ref> An official clip was released during [[San Diego Comic-Con]] in 2022,<ref>{{Cite web |title=SDCC 2022: 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Announces Stellar Guest and Recurring Voice Cast |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/sdcc-2022-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-voice-cast |access-date=November 23, 2022 |website=Marvel Entertainment |language=en |archive-date=May 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530073551/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/sdcc-2022-moon-girl-devil-dinosaur-voice-cast |url-status=live }}</ref> while the first trailer was released during the [[D23 Expo]], where the first episode was shown to the attendees.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 9, 2022 |title="Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur" Panel at New York Comic Con 2022 |url=https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2022/10/09/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-panel-at-new-york-comic-con-2022/ |access-date=January 2, 2023 |website=LaughingPlace.com |language=en-US |archive-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102022259/https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2022/10/09/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-panel-at-new-york-comic-con-2022/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=D23 Expo 2022: 'Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Trailer Reveals February 10 Premiere Date |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-trailer-premiere-date |access-date=November 23, 2022 |website=Marvel Entertainment |language=en |archive-date=November 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221123005857/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-trailer-premiere-date |url-status=live }}</ref> The series' intro was released during the [[New York Comic Con]] 2022, with the first two minutes of the season premiere also being shown during the panel.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 10, 2022 |title='Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' Brought Moon Girl Magic to New York Comic Con |url=https://www.nerdsandbeyond.com/2022/10/10/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-brought-moon-girl-magic-to-new-york-comic-con/ |access-date=January 2, 2023 |website=Nerds and Beyond |language=en |author=Julia |archive-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102022246/https://www.nerdsandbeyond.com/2022/10/10/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-brought-moon-girl-magic-to-new-york-comic-con/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":2" /> A music video for the theme song was released on November 14, 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |title='Moon Girl Magic' Music Video Released from 'Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur' |url=https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/moon-girl-magic-music-video-released-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur |access-date=November 23, 2022 |website=Marvel Entertainment |language=en |archive-date=November 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221123005857/https://www.marvel.com/articles/tv-shows/moon-girl-magic-music-video-released-from-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur |url-status=live }}</ref>

Merchandising for the show will include apparel and a series of toys produced by The World of EPI and [[Funko Pop!]] that serve as tie-ins for the series.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=February 11, 2022 |title=Disney Teams with The World of EPI for New Dolls and Toys Inspired by "The Proud Family: Louder and Prouder", "Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur" |url=https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2022/02/11/disney-world-of-epi-dolls-toys-the-proud-family-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |access-date=January 2, 2023 |website=LaughingPlace.com |language=en-US |archive-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102021519/https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2022/02/11/disney-world-of-epi-dolls-toys-the-proud-family-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Characters from ''Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur'' appear as limited-time meet-and-greet characters at the [[Avengers Campus]] at [[Disney California Adventure]] starting from February 15, 2023, in commemoration of [[Black History Month]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 19, 2023 |title="Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur" Character Meet & Greet Coming to Disney California Adventure |url=https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2023/01/19/dca-moon-girl-meet-and-greet/ |access-date=January 19, 2023 |website=LaughingPlace.com |language=en-US |archive-date=January 19, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119193157/https://www.laughingplace.com/w/news/2023/01/19/dca-moon-girl-meet-and-greet/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Moon Girl Magic Comes to Disney California Adventure Park |url=https://disneyparks.disney.go.com/blog/2023/02/moon-girl-magic-comes-to-disney-california-adventure-park/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230210024022/https://disneyparks.disney.go.com/blog/2023/02/moon-girl-magic-comes-to-disney-california-adventure-park/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=February 10, 2023 |access-date=February 15, 2023 |website=Disney Parks Blog |date=February 9, 2023 |language=en-US}}</ref> A soundtrack featuring songs from the first season, in digital and audio cassette formats, was released on January 4, 2023, by [[Walt Disney Records]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BRNL3J6S/|title=Marvel's Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur[Green Cassette]|website=[[Amazon (company)|Amazon]]|access-date=March 12, 2023|archive-date=March 13, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230313143638/https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BRNL3J6S|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Reception==
===Critical reception===
On the [[review aggregator]] website [[Rotten Tomatoes]], the first season holds an approval rating of 100%, based on 16 reviews with an average rating of 8.7/10. The site's critical consensus reads: "With dazzling visuals and punchy pacing that feel beamed straight from a child's imagination, ''Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur'' will enthrall kids while impressing animation connoisseurs."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/marvels_moon_girl_and_devil_dinosaur/s01 |access-date=February 10, 2023 |website=[[Rotten Tomatoes]] |language=en |archive-date=February 11, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230211034346/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/marvels_moon_girl_and_devil_dinosaur/s01 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Joel Keller of [[Decider (website)|''Decider'']] asserted, "''Marvel's Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur'' is a dynamic, smart, visually arresting series that has a unique girl at its center, a puppy-like dinosaur, and stories that will entertain both kids and their parents."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Keller |first=Joel |date=February 10, 2023 |title='Marvel's Moon Girl And Devil Dinosaur' Disney Channel Review: Stream It Or Skip It? |url=https://decider.com/2023/02/10/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-disney-channel-review/ |website=[[Decider (website)|Decider]] |access-date=February 12, 2023 |language=en-US |archive-date=February 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230212081823/https://decider.com/2023/02/10/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-disney-channel-review/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Matthew Aguilar of ''[[ComicBook.com]]'' gave the series a 4 out of 5, writing, "''Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur'' is an enchanting adventure that wears its heart and charm on its sleeve. The show carves out a corner of the Marvel universe all its own with personality for days and characters that leap off the screen, and any Marvel fan will regret missing out on this one-of-a-kind gem."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Aguilar |first=Matthew |date=February 11, 2023 |title=Marvel's Moon Girl and Devil Dinosaur Review: A Superhero Adventure Brimming with Heart, Humor, and Charm |url=https://comicbook.com/tv-shows/news/marvels-moon-girl-and-devil-dinosaur-review-streaming-disney-plus-animated/ |access-date=February 12, 2023 |website=Comicbook.com |language=en}}</ref>

